   211	## 5. Why 21 of the 22 violations are selected in advance
   212	
   213	Let `r` be the number of pole vertices.  The promoted leaf identities give
   214	
   215	```text
   216	td = sum_i Lambda_i,       Lambda_i >= beta.
   217	```
   218	
   219	Moreover MP4 says `Lambda_i=beta` forces `b_i=1`.  Hence:
   220	
   221	- if `r > alpha`, then `td >= r*beta > alpha*beta`;
   222	- if `r = alpha`, then `td >= alpha*beta`, and equality would force every
   223	  pole to be beta-minimal and hence every `b_i=1`;
   224	- therefore an **off-axis** row (some `b_i>=2`) with `r>=alpha` satisfies
   225	  `td > alpha*beta` before L6, a budget, or a tower is consulted.
   226	
   227	This applies to 21 of the 23 rows: all 19 type-`(2,3)` rows and both
   228	type-`(2,5)` rows have `alpha=2` and `r>=2`.  It explains 21 of the 22
   229	violations by construction.  The effective nonautomatic sample has only two
   230	rows:
   231	
   232	```text
   233	td13, type (3,4), r=2, Lambda=(4,9):  excess 5 > capacity 4  -> violates;
   234	td12, type (3,5), r=2, Lambda=(6,6):  excess 2 <= capacity 5 -> holds.
   235	```
   236	
   237	More generally, put `e_i=Lambda_i-beta >= 0`.  Then
   238	
   239	```text
   240	td <= alpha*beta
   241	    <=> r <= alpha and sum_i e_i <= (alpha-r)*beta
   242	    <=> sum_i a_i*b_i/nu_i <= 1.
   243	```
   244	
   245	This is useful as a proof reduction, but it makes the 22/23 frequency weak
   246	evidence.  The off-axis selector has preloaded almost the whole answer.
   247	
   248	The other entry filters do not secretly prove the remaining inequality:
   249	
   250	- L6/N1 is only `gcd(a,nu)=1` here.
   251	- Fixed-type N1-valid arithmetic progressions have unbounded `td`
   252	  (`SHEET6-TDUNIFORM.md:99-122`).
   253	- The printed shared budget has right side `td-1-psi`, which loosens as `td`
   254	  grows, and the pure `M=1` forest is budget-transparent
   255	  (`SHEET6-MULTIPOLE.md:61-64`).
   256	
   257	So this is selection bias, not yet a theorem disguised as L6 or the budget.
   258	
   259	### “Entire filed corpus” is also too broad
   260	
   261	Repeating the same `book_enum.entries` enumeration through L6 **before** the
   262	off-axis filter gives 43 entries: 23 off-axis plus 20 all-`b=1` entries.  The
   263	scan manually reinstates residue-A but omits the other 19 on-axis entries.
   264	Among all 20 all-`b=1` entries, 12 satisfy the bound and 8 violate it; all 20
   265	have at least one inline `BOOK-ENUM` survivor.  Their types include
   266	
   267	```text
   268	(2,3), (2,5), (2,7), (3,4), (3,7), (4,5), (5,6), (6,7),
   269	```
   270	
   271	so the complete L6 entry set has nine types after adding off-axis `(3,5)` and
   272	has maximum `alpha*beta=42`, not 15.  `BOOK-ENUM.md:60-81` records the relevant
   273	on-axis panels, while `cases/book_offaxis.py:49-51` deliberately drops them.
