xmodel/sol-reduction.out-18703-271:\begin{corollary}[the book collapses $62\to6$]\label{cor:62to6}
xmodel/sol-reduction.out-18704-272:Of the $62$ cells of the priced $\mathrm{td}=7$ class-B/C book, $56$ are T1-dead by Theorem \ref{thm:zcl}: the one class-B cell $(3,9,2,3)$ and $55$ class-C cells, removing $1636$ of the $1689$ deduplicated routes. The six survivors, all class C, genuinely pass the complete local solve, with explicit admissible solutions:
xmodel/sol-reduction.out-18705-327:At $\mu_0=1$ the corrected zero-edge equation reads $X_G=\kb_G-\nu_G w_U$, and the chain-1 handshake \eqref{eq:I3} gives $X_G=\kb_G-2$; hence $\nu_G\,w_U=2$. The cell data are $d_p=1+\nu_G$, $d_q=d_p+c$, and the arrival divisibility ($g\mid m+c-1$ at $m=1$) gives $\nu_G\mid c$, while integrality of $\kb=2d_q/(d_q-d_p)=2+2(1+\nu_G)/c$ gives $c\mid 2(1+\nu_G)$. Write $g=\nu_G\geq2$. If $g\geq3$ is odd then $\gcd(g,2(1+g))=\gcd(g,2)=1$, so $g\mid c\mid 2(1+g)$ forces $g=1$, a contradiction. If $g\geq4$ is even, write $c=gm'$; then $g\mid c\mid 2(1+g)$ and $\gcd(g,1+g)=1$ force $g\mid2$, again a contradiction. So $g=\nu_G=2$ and $w_U=1$. The candidate cells are then $c\in\{2,6\}$, i.e.\ $(3,5)$ with $M=1$, dead by MP2 (interior merges emit $M\geq2$), and $(3,9,2,3)$ with $\kb=3$. But the arrival state must have $w_U=1$, and the $w=1$ states of the priced closure carry $\lambda_{\mathrm{pre}}\in\{4,5\}$; no budget-fitting completion exists under \eqref{eq:budget}. So no class-B cell is realizable at all, before any local solve. (The cell $(3,9,2,3)$ was in addition T1-dead by Theorem \ref{thm:zcl}, its $\kb=3$ being the on-axis test $3\mid9$.)
xmodel/sol-reduction.out-18706-339:Each has $\kb=2d_q/(d_q-d_p)=6$ and even $\nu_G$, so $\gcd(\kb,\nu_G)\geq2$, violating \eqref{eq:L6}. In the $62$-cell book L6 had been a passing sanity check on every cell; under the corrected pin it becomes a working filter.
