   785	---
   786	
   787	## 3. Gap list, ranked by severity
   788	
   789	### CRITICAL 1 — the starting predicate is wrong
   790	
   791	The literal condition “\(J(f,g)=1\), not both linear-equivalent to
   792	coordinates” admits nonlinear polynomial automorphisms. It cannot start a
   793	counterexample reduction. Replace it everywhere by “\(F\) is not a
   794	polynomial automorphism.” This is not a proof gap; it is a false hypothesis
   795	translation.
   796	
   797	### CRITICAL 2 — “every counterexample has GGV-minimal data” has the wrong quantifier
   798	
   799	GGV minimizes \(\gcd(\deg P,\deg Q)\) over the set of **all**
   800	counterexamples and selects a pair attaining that global minimum. It does not
   801	make an arbitrary counterexample minimal. A proof by contradiction may work
   802	with the selected pair, but the theorem must say
   803	
   804	\[
   805	  \exists\text{ counterexample}\Longrightarrow
   806	  \exists\text{ GGV-minimal standard counterexample},
   807	\]
   808	
   809	not “every counterexample carries the selected minimal-pair data.” No
   810	argument relates the selected pair's \(td\) to that of an arbitrary original
   811	pair.
   812	
   813	### CRITICAL 3 — the GGV-to-sheet arrow is absent and the reduced GGV object is often ill-typed
   814	
   815	There is no theorem translating GGV corners/admissible chains into Sigray
   816	pole/tree decorations. The early GGV standardization is a polynomial-ring
   817	automorphism and preserves a Keller pair, but the per-family §4 reductions
   818	used by the farm pass through Laurent automorphisms and finish with a map not
   819	in \(\operatorname{Aut}\mathbf C[x,y]\). Proposition 4.3 lands at
   820	\([P,Q]=x^2\), whereas Sigray assumes a constant-Jacobian polynomial pair.
   821	
   822	One can re-normalize the selected counterexample independently in Sigray's
   823	sense, but then the GGV data are discarded. Calling this a sequential
   824	reduction is mathematically misleading.
   825	
   826	### CRITICAL 4 — no canonical universal endpoint or full-configuration landing theorem
   827	
   828	`B(td,entry)` does not occur as a defined object in the repository.
   829	The reviewed E/T/C′/J′/R/S specification does define `BOOK(s,td)` at the
   830	local marked-event tier and asserts conditional landing there. Elsewhere,
   831	“book” also denotes an aggregate of cells over entry contexts or an alleged
   832	set of full configurations. Those readings have different completeness
   833	obligations and do not define the requested universal \(B(d,E)\).
   834	
   835	A usable universal/full-configuration endpoint requires all of the following,
   836	none of which is presently stated in one theorem:
   837	
   838	1. a precise object \(B(d,E)\);
   839	2. a deterministic map from every normalized sheet configuration to a book
   840	   record;
   841	3. proof that the map terminates and is total;
   842	4. preservation of the branch, entry, and downstream context required by
   843	   every cell kill; and
   844	5. a fail-closed coverage certificate showing that no transition was skipped.
   845	
   846	The natural first repair is to turn the existing **marked-first-event landing
   847	theorem** into a typed, certified map: select the earliest resonant/
   848	\(M\ne1\) event, include its complete pole-side subtree, and explicitly type
   849	the unused branches and downstream continuation.
   850	
   851	### CRITICAL 5 — the off-axis sector has no completeness theorem
   852	
   853	[BOOK-OFFAXIS.md](BOOK-OFFAXIS.md) §10 P5 is explicit: for any
   854	\(b\ge2\) entry, the generic grid loop has no completeness certificate. At a
   855	fixed budget, reachable states can have unbounded numerator and \(M\), and
   856	the solver's proved bounds scale with them. The reported 2691 generic cells
   857	are therefore all `OPEN`; they are not an exhaustive book whose survivors
   858	have merely not yet been killed.
   859	
   860	This gap already occurs at two poles. The older parenthetical claim that a
   861	mixed all-\(\mu_e\ge2\) merge requires \(s\ge3\) is **false**:
   862	[BOOK-OFFAXIS.md](BOOK-OFFAXIS.md) §3 exhibits \(s=2\) entries with both
   863	\(b_i\ge2\) and both arrivals of multiplicity two.
   864	
   865	The \(d=7\) inversion is special. It is cap-free inside its hand-proved
   866	classification, but the final tower theorem still declares the filed route
   867	perimeter as a hypothesis. There is no analogous completed census at
   868	\(d=11\), \(d=13\), or general \(d\).
   869	
   870	### CRITICAL 6 — even the all-`b=1` “full book” omits post-jump configurations
   871	
   872	The \(w\)-closure theorem controls pure \(M=1\) ancestry. It does not control
   873	a resonant jump that emits \(M\ge2\), a subsequent \(M\ge2\) chain, and a
   874	later merge whose arrivals are all \(\mu_e\ge2\). These two-jump/mixed
   875	configurations are expressly quarantined in:
   876	
   877	- [SHEET6-MULTIPOLE.md](SHEET6-MULTIPOLE.md) §4;
   878	- [BOOK-BASH-R2.md](BOOK-BASH-R2.md) §6; and
   879	- [TEMPLATE-ATTACK.md](TEMPLATE-ATTACK.md) §§1c and 6.
   880	
   881	Thus `SHEET6-DEPTH.md` §8's “finite book for every \((s,d)\)” is an
   882	overstatement if read as a full-configuration theorem. Its own §9 gives the
   883	narrower, correct perimeter. The conditional finite local first-jump landing
   884	theorem remains valid at its stated trust perimeter; equality with an
   885	implemented full-configuration book is not proved.
   886	
   887	### CRITICAL 7 — finite-range enumeration cannot reduce JC2
   888	
   889	The implemented on-axis sweep is exactly \(6\le d\le14\). The off-axis
   890	entry census discussed in the book files is also bounded. No cited GGV result
   891	or sheet theorem supplies an upper bound on \(d\). The GGV22 “\(\ge125\) or
   892	\((72,108)\)” result is a lower-bound dichotomy in polynomial degree and says
   893	nothing that truncates topological degree.
   894	
   895	Therefore “the books cover all configurations” is **not a theorem at every
   896	\(d\)**, even before the sector omissions are considered.
   897	
   898	| Sector | What is finite in theorem form? | What is actually enumerated? | Exhaustive full configurations? |
   899	|---|---|---|---|
   900	| Entry layer, any fixed \(d\) | Necessary arithmetic entry data \(E\), conditional on T7's corrected inputs | Entry scans in bounded campaigns | **Yes for the necessary entry menu at a fixed \(d\); no claim of realizability or full routes** |
