     1	# TRANSPORT.md — the GGV–Sigray normalization transport
     2	
     3	**Status (2026-08-17): THEOREM at the pre-Laurent normalization layer.**
     4	The coordinate-cusp obstruction, exact target-degree formula, orbitwise
     5	lexicographic minimality, and signed-axis transport are proved below.  The
     6	machine gate is [cases/transport_check.py](cases/transport_check.py).
     7	
     8	**Scope.** Work over \(\mathbf C\).  This document repairs REDUCTION gap G2
     9	for the GGV-selected polynomial standard pair.  It does not turn a GGV
    10	admissible chain into a decorated Eggers–Wall tree, does not apply to the
    11	post-Laurent \([P,Q]=x^j\) objects, and does not repair book coverage or the
    12	absence of an upper bound on topological degree.
    13	
    14	## 0. Verdict
    15	
    16	There is a lossless two-chart simultaneous normal form.
    17	
    18	Let the pre-Laurent GGV pair have a common positive base corner
    19	\(A=(a,b)\), with coprime multipliers \(m,n>1\):
    20	
    21	\[
    22	 \operatorname{Supp}P\subseteq[0,ma]\times[0,mb],\qquad
    23	 \operatorname{Supp}Q\subseteq[0,na]\times[0,nb],
    24	\]
    25	
    26	and suppose the northeast corners \(mA,nA\) occur.  Put
    27	\(\alpha=\min(m,n)\), \(\beta=\max(m,n)\).  After the determinant-one target
    28	rotation
    29	
    30	\[
    31	 C(U,V)=
    32	 \begin{cases}
    33	 (U,V),&m<n,\\
    34	 (V,-U),&n<m,
    35	 \end{cases}
    36	\]
    37	
    38	the lower multiplier is first.  The resulting pair is already
    39	lexicographically minimal under every Sigray source/target polynomial
    40	equivalence.  The determinant-one source rotation
    41	
    42	\[
    43	 R(x,y)=(y,-x)
    44	\]
    45	
    46	then gives a Sigray-normalized representative with corners
    47	
    48	\[
    49	 (k_f,l_f)=\alpha(b,a),\qquad
    50	 (k_g,l_g)=\beta(b,a),
    51	\]
    52	
    53	and type \((\alpha,\beta)\).  The original GGV polygon and chain ledger are
    54	recovered exactly by the inverse rotations.
    55	
    56	A literal one-chart normal form would be the wrong statement: GGV standard
    57	orientation has \(a<b\), whereas Sigray fixes \(l_f\le k_f\).  The signed axis
    58	rotation is therefore part of the theorem, not cosmetic notation.
    59	
    60	The load-bearing new fact is the following coordinate-cusp theorem.  It has
    61	a self-contained locally-nilpotent-derivation proof; weighted
    62	Jung–van der Kulk is not an additional dependency.
    63	
    64	## 1. The coordinate-cusp theorem
    65	
    66	For positive weights \(w=(r,s)\), write
    67	
    68	\[
    69	 \deg_w\!\left(\sum a_{ij}U^iV^j\right)
    70	   =\max_{a_{ij}\ne0}(ri+sj)
    71	\]
    72	
    73	and let \(\operatorname{in}_w h\) be the part of maximal \(w\)-degree.  A
    74	polynomial \(h\in\mathbf C[U,V]\) is a **coordinate** if it is one component
    75	of a polynomial automorphism.
    76	
    77	### Theorem 1.1 (coordinate-cusp exclusion)
    78	
    79	Let \(r,s>1\) be coprime and let \(c,d\in\mathbf C^*\).  If \(h\) is a
    80	coordinate, then
    81	
    82	\[
    83	 \operatorname{in}_{r,s}h\notin
    84	 \bigl(d^rU^s-c^sV^r\bigr).
    85	 \tag{1.1}
    86	\]
    87	
    88	Equivalently,
    89	
    90	\[
    91	 \operatorname{in}_{r,s}h(cZ^r,dZ^s)\ne0.
    92	 \tag{1.2}
    93	\]
    94	
    95	#### Proof
    96	
    97	Choose a coordinate mate \(k\) with \(J(h,k)=\lambda\in\mathbf C^*\), and
    98	set
    99	
   100	\[
   101	 \partial=J(h,-).
   102	\]
   103	
   104	In the coordinates \((h,k)\), \(\partial=\lambda\,\partial/\partial k\).
   105	Thus \(\partial\) is a locally nilpotent derivation (LND).
   106	
   107	Put \(\bar h=\operatorname{in}_{r,s}h\).  The highest filtered homogeneous
   108	part of \(\partial\) is
   109	
   110	\[
   111	 D=J(\bar h,-).
   112	\]
   113	
   114	This \(D\) is also locally nilpotent.  Here is the filtered argument in the
   115	form needed below.  The derivation \(D\) is homogeneous for the
   116	\((r,s)\)-grading.  If it were not locally nilpotent, some homogeneous
   117	\(q\) would have \(D^j(q)\ne0\) for every \(j\ge0\).  Inductively,
   118	\(D^j(q)\) is then the highest homogeneous part of \(\partial^j(q)\).
   119	Consequently no \(\partial^j(q)\) could vanish, contradicting local
   120	nilpotence of \(\partial\).  Also \(D\ne0\), because \(\bar h\) is
   121	nonconstant in characteristic zero.
   122	
   123	For a nonzero element \(q\), define its LND degree
   124	
   125	\[
   126	 \delta_D(q)=\max\{j:D^j(q)\ne0\}.
   127	\]
   128	
   129	The Leibniz formula in a characteristic-zero domain gives
   130	
   131	\[
   132	 \delta_D(q_1q_2)=\delta_D(q_1)+\delta_D(q_2).
   133	 \tag{1.3}
   134	\]
   135	
   136	Indeed, if the two degrees are \(e_1,e_2\), the only nonzero term of
   137	\(D^{e_1+e_2}(q_1q_2)\) is
   138	\(\binom{e_1+e_2}{e_1}D^{e_1}q_1D^{e_2}q_2\).  In particular
   139	\(\ker D\) is factorially closed: if a product lies in \(\ker D\), both
   140	factors lie in \(\ker D\).
   141	
   142	Assume for contradiction that
   143	
   144	\[
   145	 \bar h=Bq,\qquad B=d^rU^s-c^sV^r.
   146	\]
   147	
   148	Since \(D(\bar h)=J(\bar h,\bar h)=0\), factorial closure gives
   149	\(B\in\ker D\).  Hence
   150	
   151	\[
   152	 s d^rU^{s-1}D(U)=r c^sV^{r-1}D(V).
   153	 \tag{1.4}
   154	\]
   155	
   156	Coprimality of the powers of \(U\) and \(V\) in the polynomial ring implies
   157	
   158	\[
   159	 V^{r-1}\mid D(U),\qquad U^{s-1}\mid D(V).
   160	 \tag{1.5}
   161	\]
   162	
   163	Neither \(D(U)\) nor \(D(V)\) is zero: by (1.4), one vanishes if and only if
   164	the other does, which would give \(D=0\).  Set
   165	\(p=\delta_D(U)\) and \(q_0=\delta_D(V)\).  Since
   166	\(\delta_D(Da)=\delta_D(a)-1\) whenever \(D(a)\ne0\), (1.5) and (1.3) give
   167	
   168	\[
   169	 p-1\ge(r-1)q_0,\qquad q_0-1\ge(s-1)p.
   170	\]
   171	
   172	Adding and rearranging yields
   173	
   174	\[
   175	 (s-2)p+(r-2)q_0\le-2,
   176	\]
   177	
   178	which is impossible because \(r,s\ge2\) and \(p,q_0\ge0\).  This proves
   179	(1.1).
   180	
   181	Finally, the homomorphism
   182	
   183	\[
   184	 \mathbf C[U,V]\longrightarrow\mathbf C[Z],\qquad
   185	 U\longmapsto cZ^r,\quad V\longmapsto dZ^s
   186	\]
   187	
   188	has kernel \((B)\).  One way to see this is that \(B\) is the primitive
   189	irreducible binomial for the coprime monomial curve, and both the quotient
   190	and its image \(\mathbf C[Z^r,Z^s]\) are one-dimensional domains.  Thus
   191	(1.1) and (1.2) are equivalent. \(\square\)
   192	
   193	### Corollary 1.2 (exact cusp substitution degree)
   194	
   195	If \(H\) is a nonconstant homogeneous polynomial of ordinary degree \(D\),
   196	then every coordinate \(h\) satisfies
   197	
   198	\[
   199	 \deg h(cH^r,dH^s)=D\deg_{r,s}h.
   200	 \tag{1.6}
   201	\]
   202	
   203	#### Proof
   204	
   205	If \(e=\deg_{r,s}h\), every term of the top face becomes a scalar multiple
   206	of \(H^e\).  The scalar is nonzero by Theorem 1.1.  Every lower face has
   207	ordinary degree less than \(De\). \(\square\)
   208	
   209	## 2. Orbitwise minimality of a rectangular cusp pair
   210	
   211	### Theorem 2.1 (exact source/target degree transport)
   212	
   213	Let \(1<r<s\) be coprime.  Suppose \(P,Q\in\mathbf C[x,y]\) have, for some
   214	positive integers \(a,b\),
   215	
   216	\[
   217	\begin{aligned}
   218	 \operatorname{Supp}P&\subseteq[0,ra]\times[0,rb],
   219	 & [x^{ra}y^{rb}]P&=c\ne0,\\
   220	 \operatorname{Supp}Q&\subseteq[0,sa]\times[0,sb],
   221	 & [x^{sa}y^{sb}]Q&=d\ne0.
   222	\end{aligned}
   223	\tag{2.1}
   224	\]
   225	
   226	For a source automorphism \(L=(u,v)\), put
   227	
   228	\[
   229	 D_L=a\deg u+b\deg v,qquad H=u_+^av_+^b,
   230	\tag{2.2}
   231	\]
   232	
   233	where \(u_+,v_+\) are the top ordinary homogeneous parts.  Then, for every
   234	target coordinate \(h(U,V)\),
   235	
   236	\[
   237	 \deg h(P\circ L,Q\circ L)=D_L\deg_{r,s}h.
   238	\tag{2.3}
   239	\]
   240	
   241	If \(K=(h_1,h_2)\) is any target automorphism, then
   242	
   243	\[
   244	 \bigl(\deg h_1(P\circ L,Q\circ L),
   245	       \deg h_2(P\circ L,Q\circ L)\bigr)
   246	 \ge_{\rm lex}
   247	 \bigl(r(a+b),s(a+b)\bigr).
   248	\tag{2.4}
   249	\]
   250	
   251	Consequently, if \((P,Q)\) is a counterexample with its \(r\)-component
   252	first, it is almost normalized in Sigray's source/target equivalence class.
   253	
   254	#### Proof
   255	
   256	Every source coordinate is nonconstant, so \(\deg u,\deg v\ge1\) and
   257	
   258	\[
   259	 D_L\ge a+b.
   260	\tag{2.5}
   261	\]
   262	
   263	The northeast monomial in each rectangle is the unique term maximizing the
   264	positive degree functional
   265	\((i,j)\mapsto i\deg u+j\deg v\).  Therefore
   266	
   267	\[
   268	 (P\circ L)_+=cH^r,\qquad (Q\circ L)_+=dH^s,
   269	\tag{2.6}
   270	\]
   271	
   272	and their degrees are \(rD_L,sD_L\).  For a target polynomial \(h\), only
   273	its maximal \((r,s)\)-face can contribute in ordinary degree
   274	\(D_L\deg_{r,s}h\).  Cancellation at that degree would say
   275	
   276	\[
   277	 \operatorname{in}_{r,s}h(cZ^r,dZ^s)=0,
   278	\]
   279	
   280	which Theorem 1.1 forbids when \(h\) is a coordinate.  This proves (2.3).
   281	
   282	Every nonconstant polynomial has \((r,s)\)-degree at least \(r\), so the
   283	first degree in (2.4) is at least \(rD_L\ge r(a+b)\).  Equality throughout
   284	forces \(D_L=a+b\) and \(\deg_{r,s}h_1=r\).  Since \(r<s\), the latter
   285	identity forces
   286	
   287	\[
   288	 h_1=\gamma U+\gamma_0,\qquad \gamma\ne0.
   289	\]
   290	
   291	Now \(J(h_1,h_2)=\gamma\,\partial h_2/\partial V\in\mathbf C^*\), so
   292	\(h_2\) has a nonzero term linear in \(V\) and
   293	\(\deg_{r,s}h_2\ge s\).  Formula (2.3) gives the required second-coordinate
   294	bound.  Thus no source/target equivalent representative has a smaller
   295	ordered degree pair. \(\square\)
   296	
   297	Two points are worth isolating.
   298	
   299	1. The theorem is stronger than global GGV minimality.  It proves minimum
   300	   degree inside this pair's entire Sigray equivalence class directly.
   301	2. Ordinary nondivisibility of \(rD_L,sD_L\) alone would not be enough:
   302	   \(P^s-Q^r\) can cancel.  Theorem 1.1 is exactly what excludes such a
   303	   cancellation in a *coordinate* of a target automorphism.
   304	
   305	## 3. The GGV-to-Sigray normalization theorem
   306	
   307	### Theorem 3.1 (two-chart simultaneous normal form)
   308	
   309	Assume JC2 is false and select the globally minimal standard GGV pair supplied
   310	by the published GGV theorem.  Use the subrectangular conclusion in its exact
   311	form: there are coprime \(m,n>1\), a positive integral base
   312	\(A=(a,b)\) with \(a<b\), proportional support rectangles as in (2.1), and
   313	both northeast corners occur.  Before proceeding, harmlessly scale one target
   314	component so that \([P,Q]=1\); this does not change any support, degree, corner,
   315	minimality, or chain datum used here.
   316	
   317	Define \(C\), \(R\), \(\alpha\), and \(\beta\) as in Section 0 and set
   318	
   319	\[
   320	 (f,g)=C\circ(P,Q)\circ R.
   321	\tag{3.1}
   322	\]
   323	
   324	Then:
   325	
   326	1. \((f,g)\) is the same nonautomorphic Keller map up to Sigray
   327	   equivalence, and its topological degree is unchanged;
   328	2. \((f,g)\) is almost normalized;
   329	3. its Newton polygons lie in the rectangles with positive northeast
   330	   corners
   331	   \[
   332	   (k_f,l_f)=\alpha(b,a),\qquad
   333	   (k_g,l_g)=\beta(b,a);
   334	   \]
   335	4. these corners satisfy
   336	   \[
   337	   \frac{k_f}{k_g}=\frac{l_f}{l_g}=\frac\alpha\beta,
   338	   \quad k_f<k_g,\quad l_f<l_g,\quad l_f<k_f,
   339	   \quad \frac{k_g}{k_f}=\frac\beta\alpha\notin\mathbf N;
   340	   \]
   341	5. hence \((f,g)\) is Sigray-normalized of type \((\alpha,\beta)\).
   342	
   343	#### Proof
   344	
   345	The target map \(C\) is either the identity or the determinant-one rotation
   346	\((U,V)\mapsto(V,-U)\); it sorts the two multipliers without changing the
   347	normalized base polygon.  Theorem 2.1 proves almost-normalized minimality.
   348	The source map \(R(x,y)=(y,-x)\) also has determinant one and preserves total
   349	degrees, so the rotated representative remains almost normalized and keeps
   350	Jacobian one.  On exponents it sends \((i,j)\) to \((j,i)\), giving the two
   351	displayed rectangles.  The five numerical assertions follow from
   352	\(0<a<b\), \(1<\alpha<\beta\), and
   353	\(\gcd(\alpha,\beta)=1\).  They are precisely Sigray's rectangular
   354	normal-form conditions and type definition.  Polynomial automorphisms
   355	preserve the function-field extension and noninvertibility. \(\square\)
   356	
   357	For this selected-pair branch, Theorem 3.1 bypasses the Abhyankar inputs used
   358	by Sigray Lemma 2.1: GGV supplies the rectangles, and Theorem 2.1 supplies
   359	lexicographic minimality.  Sigray Notations 2.1–2.4 are used to identify the
   360	resulting object as normalized and to name its type.  Sigray's theorem for an
   361	arbitrary counterexample remains unchanged.
   362	
   363	## 4. Exact transport of the GGV ledger
   364	
   365	Let \(R^*H=H(y,-x)\), and write
   366	
   367	\[
   368	 T(i,j)=(j,i),\qquad T(\rho,\sigma)=(\sigma,\rho).
   369	\tag{4.1}
   370	\]
   371	
   372	For every polynomial \(H\), every direction \(w=(\rho,\sigma)\), and every
   373	edge with the usual GGV endpoint convention,
   374	
   375	\[
   376	\begin{aligned}
   377	 \operatorname{Supp}(R^*H)&=T\operatorname{Supp}(H),\\
   378	 N(R^*H)&=T N(H),\\
   379	 v_{Tw}(R^*H)&=v_w(H),\\
   380	 \ell_{Tw}(R^*H)&=R^*\ell_w(H),\\
   381	 \operatorname{st}_{Tw}(R^*H)&=T\operatorname{en}_w(H),\\
   382	 \operatorname{en}_{Tw}(R^*H)&=T\operatorname{st}_w(H).
   383	\end{aligned}
   384	\tag{4.2}
   385	\]
   386	
   387	The first four identities follow term by term from
   388	
   389	\[
   390	 x^iy^j\longmapsto(-1)^j x^jy^i,qquad
   391	 (\sigma,\rho)\mathbin\cdot(j,i)=(\rho,\sigma)\mathbin\cdot(i,j).
   392	\]
   393	
   394	The last two reflect the fact that \(T\) reverses orientation on the exponent
   395	lattice.  They can also be checked from GGV's defining inequality
   396	\(w\times(\operatorname{en}-\operatorname{st})>0\).
   397	
   398	In the native repo convention an oriented edge stores
   399	\((A_h,A'_h)=(\operatorname{en}_w,\operatorname{st}_w)\).  There are two
   400	closely related tuples after transport, and they must not be conflated.  The
   401	**label-preserving rational ledger** is the involution
   402	
   403	\[
   404	 (A_h,A'_h;(\rho_h,\sigma_h))
   405	 \longmapsto
   406	 (TA_h,TA'_h;(\sigma_h,\rho_h)).
   407	\tag{4.3}
   408	\]
   409	
   410	The **orientation-preserving endpoint tuple** of the transported leading
   411	face is instead
   412	
   413	\[
   414	 (\operatorname{en}_w,\operatorname{st}_w;w)
   415	 \longmapsto
   416	 (T\operatorname{st}_w,T\operatorname{en}_w;Tw)
   417	 =(TA'_h,TA_h;Tw).
   418	\tag{4.4}
   419	\]
   420	
   421	Thus (4.3) retains the campaign names while (4.4) retains the GGV
   422	`en`/`st` orientation.  In particular, if
   423	\(w\mathbin\times(A_h-A'_h)>0\), then
   424	\((Tw)\mathbin\times(TA'_h-TA_h)>0\), whereas the pointwise ordered pair in
   425	(4.3) has the opposite cross-product sign.
   426	
   427	The GGV5 certificate fraction is unchanged:
   428	
   429	\[
   430	 \frac{\rho_h+\sigma_h}{v_{\rho_h,\sigma_h}(A_h)}
   431	 =
   432	 \frac{\sigma_h+\rho_h}{v_{\sigma_h,\rho_h}(TA_h)}.
   433	\tag{4.5}
   434	\]
   435	
   436	Accordingly its reduced \((p_h,q_h)\) tag is unchanged.  Target rotation
   437	\(C\) only swaps component labels and scalar signs; the normalized base
   438	corners are unchanged because the two component corners are \(mA_h,nA_h\).
   439	Equations (4.1)–(4.5), together with the component label bit, are an explicit
   440	inverse pair of maps.  No native GGV datum is discarded.
   441	
   442	There are two necessary type cautions.
   443	
   444	- Transposition reverses cyclic direction order and swaps `st`/`en`.  A
   445	  direction-ordered display must therefore be reindexed.
   446	- A GGV Laurent corner \((a/l,b)\) may transpose to \((b,a/l)\), which is
   447	  not represented by the repo's x-fraction-only `Corner(a,l,b)` class.  The
   448	  Sigray-side object is a rational valuation ledger, not a claim that all
   449	  fourteen GGV5 admissibility clauses have been re-certified in their native
   450	  chart.  Applying \(T^{-1}\) recovers the certified GGV chain exactly.
   451	
   452	The Sigray representative is formed at the polynomial starting pair, before
   453	**any** GGV5/GGV22 Laurent operation.  Later rational chain corners are
   454	transported only as stored, exactly recoverable ledger metadata.  In
   455	particular, the final maps involving \(x\mapsto x^{-1}\) are not polynomial
   456	source automorphisms and the Proposition 4.3 endpoints have bracket \(x^2\),
   457	not a nonzero constant.  Those reduced strip polygons are not Sigray inputs.
   458	
   459	Finally, (4.2) is not a corner-to-tree functor.  It allows the original GGV
   460	ledger to coexist losslessly with the Eggers–Wall data attached to the
   461	explicit Sigray representative.  It does not say that corner data alone
   462	determine pole status, fiber tags, residual \(g\)-cancellations, or Sigray's
   463	decorations.
   464	
   465	## 5. Exact machine instances
