   106	Proof. Case IV's hypothesis is F = (0,y) ∉ V_{1,a} ∪ V_{2,a} (p. 50).
   107	By Def 3.4 (p. 11), (0,y) ∈ V_{2,a} iff (0,y) = I_P(O(P,P*)) for two
   108	punctures, i.e. iff two y-side punctures have contact O(P,P*) = 0
   109	(x-side pairs give O = −1, not a vertex; cross-component pairs likewise —
   110	Def 3.2, St 3.3). So in case IV every pair of y-side punctures has contact
   111	> 0: all y-side Puiseux series share their first coefficient, i.e.
   112	p_{(0,y)} = ⊖(η−c)^{k_f} has a single root (Prop 3.1(∗∗), p. 14, at level
   113	0), and every y-ray runs THROUGH the first path vertex G = (0,y)+c above
   114	the root. No ray separates from the path at (0,y); a branch-at-(0,y) cv
   115	vertex would need one. ∎
   116	
   117	Remarks.
   118	- Under the LITERAL Y-definition λ_{(0,y)} would instead be the whole
   119	  y-side cv mass (every y-ray passes I_P(0)) — the E9 double-count, under
   120	  which St 9.5 is false as printed; either way no NEW unit appears at the
   121	  root. The lemma's hoped-for content does not exist in any reading.
   122	- LR1 also re-derives why the terminal ψ is sharp: single root ⇒
   123	  k_f = deg p_{(0,y)} = mult(p_{(0,y)}, c) = deg p_G (St 3.17(i), p. 18),
   124	  so k_f/l_f = deg p_G/d_F = R EXACTLY — the AF3 §5 ψ-upgrade blocker,
   125	  now with the exact equality forced rather than merely unobstructed. In
   126	  particular R ∈ N for all 8 classes (R ∈ {3,4}) and ψ = R − 1.
   127	- Contrapositive (the lemma's true, useless content): λ_root ≥ 1 DOES hold
   128	  whenever (0,y) ∈ V_{2,a} — a second root direction carries g-finite
   129	  punctures (no second pole cluster in single-pole configs; poles are one
   130	  cluster) whose cv mass ≥ 1 charges the root slot. But (0,y) ∈ V_{2,a} is
   131	  exactly NOT case IV: the sets are complementary. This retroactively
   132	  double-kills SF1-type case-I/II/III root terminations (H3 §7) — already
   133	  vacuous in the pinned book (their entries are AF3-entry-dead) — since a
   134	  V_2-root termination pays #(root directions) − 1 extra units.
   135	
   136	## 3. The x-side quantum: one cv vertex, κ_G = 1, for all 8
   137	
   138	**Lemma LR2.** For any survivor-class carrier (all have ψ ≥ 2, Σλ = 2,
   139	slack ≤ 1): the x-component of T_a carries EXACTLY ONE cv vertex G, with
   140	κ_G = 1 and π_G ∈ [R, R + slack]; equivalently, all l_f x-side Puiseux
   141	series form a single cluster (pairwise contact ≥ π_G ≥ R) with no
   142	characteristic exponent below π_G, and both p- and g-patterns unsplit
   143	below π_G on that ray in the slack-0 case (where π_G = R exactly).
   144	
   145	Proof. By §1 items 1-5, (22) gives td − 1 ≥ Σλ + Σ_{x-cv vertices}κ(π−1).
   146	Each x-side cv vertex costs ≥ ψ = R−1 ≥ 2 (§1 item 5 + integrality); two
   147	would cost ≥ 2ψ > ψ + 1 ≥ ψ + slack = (td−1) − Σλ. So one vertex;
   148	κ_G ≥ 2 would likewise cost ≥ 2(R−1) > ψ + slack. A split of the x-side
   149	f-tree below height π_G would put punctures in ≥ 2 subtrees with no common
   150	vertex above the split, hence ≥ 2 cv vertices: excluded. In the slack-0
   151	case κ_G(π_G−1) = ψ exactly forces π_G = R = k_f/l_f = k_g/l_g (Lemma
   152	2.1(ii)), which by the slope law needs deg p_{·} ≡ l_f and deg p_{g,·} ≡
   153	l_g below G (any early split lowers a slope and pushes π_G up). ∎
   154	
   155	Note the pleasant mechanism: on the x-ray, d_{f−a}(u) = k_f − u·l_f and
   156	d_g(u) = k_g − u·l_g vanish at the SAME height k_f/l_f = k_g/l_g — the
   157	normalization (Lemma 2.1(ii)) makes f and g exhaust together on the
   158	x-side, which is exactly Prop 7.2's requirement (cv vertices live in
   159	T^0 ∩ {d_g = 0}). The x-side charge is the St 9.4 ψ, now localized: it is
   160	the unique d = 0 point of the single x-cluster.
   161	
