    31	## 1. The objects
    32	
    33	At a `ν >= 2` class-C merge, R2.2 fixes (L5/L6):
    34	
    35	```text
    36	p = η^ε · Π_i F_i(x)^{p_i},        q = η · Π_i F_i(x) · Π_r G_r(x),
    37	x = η^ν,
    38	```
    39	
    40	with `F_i, G_r` monic in `x` (linear factors `x − a_i` for split
    41	orbits; higher-degree monic blocks for unsplit orbit packets — the
    42	F1 row's quadratic is one), multiplicities `p_i` the arrival `μ_e`
    43	and NE `m_j` values, `ε` the zero-slot exponent, and the q-extras
    44	`G_r`. Degrees: `d_p = ε + ν·Σ p_i f_i`, `d_q = 1 + ν·Q̂` where
    45	`Q̂ = deg_x(Π F_i · Π G_r)` and `f_i = deg F_i`. The Prop. 8.1(iv)
    46	equation is the Fuchs relation (L3):
    47	
    48	```text
    49	d_p · p · q′ − d_q · p′ · q = d_q · C · p,     C ≠ 0.
    50	```
    51	
    52	The coefficient data is `(F_i, G_r)` modulo (i) permutation of equal-
    53	multiplicity orbits and (ii) the scaling torus `η ↦ λη`
    54	(`a ↦ λ^ν a` on orbit values) — "orbit permutation and the allowed
    55	scaling" of the conjecture.
    56	
    57	## 2. The reduction (Lemma M2) and the automatic cancellation (Lemma M1)
    58	
    59	**Lemma M2 (the `x`-reduction).** Dividing (L3) by `p` and clearing,
    60	the equation is equivalent to the polynomial identity in `x` alone:
    61	
    62	```text
    63	F(x) := (d_p − d_q·ε)·G(x)
    64	        + ν·x·[ d_p·G′(x) − d_q·Σ_i p_i·(G/F_i)(x)·F_i′(x) ]
    65	      = d_q · C          (constant),
    66	where G = Π_i F_i · Π_r G_r  (monic, degree Q̂).
    67	```
    68	
    69	*Proof.* `p′/p = ε/η + Σ_i p_i·ν·η^{ν−1}·F_i′(x)/F_i(x)`; `q/η = G`
    70	and `η^{ν−1}·q/F_i = x·(G/F_i)` are polynomials; every surviving
    71	term sits in exponents `≡ 0 (mod ν)`. Machine block A verifies the
    72	reduction against the direct (L3) expansion on every certificate row
    73	and on random schemas. ∎
    74	
    75	**Lemma M1 (leading coefficient vanishes identically).** The
    76	`x^{Q̂}`-coefficient of `F` is
    77	
    78	```text
    79	(d_p − d_q·ε) + ν·(d_p·Q̂ − d_q·Σ p_i f_i)
    80	  = d_p·(1 + ν·Q̂) − d_q·(ε + ν·Σ p_i f_i) = d_p·d_q − d_q·d_p = 0.
    81	```
    82	
    83	So `F` has degree `<= Q̂ − 1` automatically — the Fuchs degree
    84	identity, not a constraint. ∎ (machine block B, full lattice)
    85	
    86	**Corollary (the square system).** The identity `F ≡ d_q·C` imposes
    87	exactly the vanishing of the `x^1 … x^{Q̂−1}` coefficients — `Q̂ − 1`
    88	equations — on the `Q̂` orbit-block coefficients modulo the 1-dim
    89	scaling torus: **`Q̂ − 1` equations in `Q̂ − 1` essential unknowns.**
    90	`F(0) = d_q·C` defines `C`; `C ≠ 0` is the open guard (L8). The
    91	`x^j`-equation is homogeneous of degree `Q̂ − j` in the orbit data,
    92	so the projective Bézout bound is `Π_{j=1}^{Q̂−1}(Q̂ − j) =
    93	(Q̂ − 1)!`.
    94	
    95	## 3. Theorem NF-M-core
    96	
    97	**Theorem.** *For every fixed `ν >= 2` discrete merge schema, the
    98	solution set of the Prop. 8.1(iv) equation modulo permutation ×
    99	scaling is the algebraic set of the square system of §2 — an
   100	explicitly computable ideal (the certificate type data). If the set
   101	is 0-dimensional modulo scaling, it consists of at most `(Q̂ − 1)!`
   102	types; two solutions of the same type with the same emitted frame
   103	have identical chain/E5F/tower futures (Lemma M3). If it is empty
   104	under the guards, the schema carries an impossibility certificate.
   105	If a component is positive-dimensional, locality is NOT claimed and
   106	the component is retained whole in `Omega` (NF-M-OB1).*
   107	
   108	**Lemma M3 (future-locality on 0-dimensional types).** A later edge
   109	observes the merged chart through: the emitted frame (discrete), the
   110	degrees (discrete), `C` (a scaling-covariant function of the type),
   111	and the orbit values via continuations (L9). Two same-type solutions
   112	differ by an element of permutation × scaling; scaling is the chart
   113	coordinate change `η ↦ λη`, under which the entire downstream system
   114	(L1–L9 at all later vertices) is covariant, and permutation of
   115	equal-multiplicity orbits is a relabelling of attachment slots.
   116	Hence the futures are identical modulo the same symmetry. ∎ —
   117	This argument NEEDS the 0-dimensional hypothesis: inside a
   118	positive-dimensional component two solutions need not be
   119	symmetry-related, and a later edge's own solve takes the arriving
   120	orbit value as a parameter whose variation is observable (resultant
   121	conditions); hence OB1's retention, matching the conjecture's
   122	"retain every coefficient/root label that a later edge can observe".
   123	
   124	**Type-set size per (entry, budget).** §2.2 gives the finite schema
   125	menu (mults bounded by `μ_e | M_e`, `r_0 <= m`, `k <= b`, the NE
   126	inequalities); per schema the bound is `(Q̂ − 1)!` with
   127	`Q̂ <= r_0 + k + x` bounded by the same data. So the total type
   128	count is computable from (entry, budget). Observed instances are far
   129	below the bound: every td-7 certificate row has EXACTLY ONE type.
