    36	## 1. The general leaf table (page-cited derivation)
    37	
    38	Setting: (f,g) a normalized counterexample of type (alpha,beta) (Not 2.4
    39	p. 9: (k_f,k_g) reduced; Lemma 2.1 => 2 <= alpha < beta, gcd(alpha,beta)=1
    40	by definition of type; alpha >= 2 by Lemma 2.1(iv) k_g/k_f not in N*).
    41	F in T_a,pole with multiplicity data (Not 5.3, p. 26): type, (Dg,D), nu,
    42	(deg p, deg p_g). Write P := deg p_F, Pg := deg p_g,F.
    43	
    44	- **(R1) Ratio pin** (St 5.2(i), p. 26; from Prop 5.3(ii)/(vii), p. 25):
    45	  D/Dg = P/Pg = alpha/beta. With gcd(alpha,beta)=1 and D,Dg,P,Pg in N*:
    46	      (D, Dg) = a*(alpha,beta),  (P, Pg) = b*(alpha,beta),  a,b in N*.
    47	- **(R2) nu-menu** (St 5.2(ii), p. 26; from Prop 5.4, p. 26):
    48	      (A) nu | alpha and nu | Pg - 1 = b*beta - 1,   or
    49	      (B) nu | beta  and nu | P  - 1 = b*alpha - 1.
    50	  In particular nu <= beta, gcd(nu, b) = 1, and gcd(nu, beta) = 1 in case
    51	  (A), gcd(nu, alpha) = 1 in case (B) (both from nu | b*(other) - 1).
    52	- **(R3) Lambda and td** (Prop 5.6 (19), p. 27; Prop 5.8 (20), p. 28):
    53	      Lambda(F) = Dg*P/nu = a*b*alpha*beta/nu;   td = sum over T_a,pole.
    54	  SINGLE POLE: Lambda = td exactly. Integrality of Lambda is automatic
    55	  (nu | alpha or nu | beta).
    56	- **(R4) Lower bound** (Prop 5.7, p. 27): Lambda >= beta, so beta <= td.
    57	- Entry Q-datum (St 9.1, p. 48): kap-bar = D + Dg = a*(alpha+beta);
    58	  rho = D/P = a/b; entry nu = nu; pcontent = P = b*alpha.
    59	
    60	This is exactly the enumeration behind table (23) (Prop 9.1, pp. 45-47,
    61	re-derived row-by-row by the engine gate for Lambda <= 6): the thesis's
    62	possibility lists "(D,Dg) = (2,3),(4,6),(6,9); (P,Pg) = (2,3),(4,6),(6,9)"
    63	are the a- and b-multiples, and its nu-eliminations are (R2)+(R3).
    64	No other constraint on the multiplicity data is printed anywhere in §5.
