   197	## 5. Budget asymptotics (question (b)) — the honest negative
   198	
   199	The BOLD-mission premise was: forced lambda-spend per chain ~ c*Lambda with
   200	c > 1 would kill all large td. **This is REFUTED, by mechanism, not just by
   201	scan.**
   202	
   203	- **Where spend comes from** (AF2/SHEET6-AF2 §2): only EXTRA p-roots (k
   204	  orbits, the IIb 0-root, III's forced second orbit) are priced. The IIa_0
   205	  cell (k = 0: p = (eta^nu_F - c^nu_F)^mu, ALL p-roots in the descending
   206	  orbit) prices at lambda = 0 for ANY l >= 0, i.e. the q-side pattern
   207	  q = eta*q~(eta^nu_F) may carry arbitrarily many extra nu_F-orbits FREE:
   208	  q-roots are not p_F-roots, create no directions (St 3.18 prices
   209	  p-directions only), hence no Y(F)-mass. This is the thesis's own printed
   210	  lambda = 0 move (9.6(v)).
   211	- **Free kap-collapse**: an IIa_0 step sends kap -> kap_F = (kap+n)/nu_G
   212	  where n solves mu*nu_F/((l+1)nu_F+1) = mu(rho+n)/(kap+n). The l-freedom
   213	  makes the reachable kap_F set rich; in particular kap_F < nu_F (case-IV
   214	  capability, Prop 9.3 (j)) is reachable in ONE free step from entries
   215	  with small kap. Since kap = a(alpha+beta) does NOT grow with b (= M =
   216	  td*nu/(a*alpha*beta)), the fixed-(alpha,beta,a) progression families of
   217	  §3 keep entry kap bounded while td -> infinity.
   218	- **The td=9 exhibit** (hand-verified, engine-independent, every promoted
   219	  constraint checked exactly):
   220	      entry (3,4), a=1, b=3, nu=4 [4|4, 4|8; Lambda=9]:
   221	        Q = (3,9,4,3,7), rho = 1/3, M = pin = gcd(9,12) = 3, N1 ok
   222	      --mu=3, IIa_0, l=1, nu_F=4: dp=12, dq=9, ratio 4/3 = 3(1/3+n)/(7+n)
   223	        => n = 5 == -kap (mod 4); kap_F = 12/4 = 3; D_F/i = 4; lambda = 0-->
   224	      child (1/3, 4, 3, 3), M_F = gcd(12,9) = 3, N1 ok
   225	      TERMINAL case IV: (j) 3 < 4; (k) d_F = P(1/3+4-3)/4 = P/3 in N;
   226	        (l) R = 3 > 1; (m) M/R = 1 in N*; psi = 2;
   227	        (25): Sum lambda = 0 <= td-1-psi = 6.  SLACK 6.
   228	  A td = 9 single-pole candidate passes the ENTIRE promoted kill set with
   229	  zero spend and slack 6. No budget refinement (any psi < R bound, any
   230	  ceil upgrade) can touch a Sum-lambda = 0 chain.
   231	- **Forced-spend scaling answer**: the minimum spend to reach a consistent
   232	  IV terminal is O(1) (often 0) on the cheap families, while the budget
   233	  td-2 grows linearly. The inequality points the WRONG WAY and worsens
   234	  with td. The only budget-driven closures are small-td accidents
   235	  (td <= 6: kap-collapse cells happen to be unsolvable or M_F = 1 there;
   236	  at td = 4 every IV hit dies by psi because the budget is 2).
