   238	## 6. What is actually provable (theorem + perimeter)
   239	
   240	**Theorem TDU (td-uniform single-pole ENTRY exclusion).** Let (f,g) be a
   241	normalized counterexample with a single pole vertex (T_a,pole = {F}),
   242	td := td(f,g) = Lambda(F). Then, with trust perimeter = the printed
   243	statements Prop 4.2 (p. 19), Prop 5.1 (pp. 23-24), Props 5.3-5.8
   244	(pp. 25-28), St 5.2 (p. 26), Not 5.2/5.3/8.1 (pp. 24, 26, 39), Prop 8.4
   245	(p. 44) — the AF3 pin perimeter, all H1-tier; NO H2/H3/H4/H5, no AF2, no
   246	chain analysis:
   247	1. The multiplicity data of F is (D,Dg) = a(alpha,beta), (P,Pg) =
   248	   b(alpha,beta) with the (R2) nu-congruences and td = a*b*alpha*beta/nu;
   249	   and M_F = b.
   250	2. If b = 1, contradiction (Prop 8.4). Hence every single-pole
   251	   configuration with (deg p_F, deg p_g,F) primitive is excluded, at
   252	   every td.
   253	3. **If td is PRIME, every single-pole configuration is excluded**: the
   254	   Lambda = td leaf table is exactly {(alpha,td) : alpha | td-1,
   255	   nu = alpha, a = b = 1}, all with b = 1. (§2 proof; mechanically
   256	   re-verified for the 11 primes <= 40.)
   257	4. [+H5a] gcd(a, nu) = 1.
   258	
