   838	
   839	The current local books also retain a type-\((2,3)\), `td=9`, three-pole
   840	residue packet of total pole mass `3/2`; after the two bash rounds the panel
   841	is reduced to the residue template rather than killed
   842	([`BOOK-BASH-R2.md:199-232`](../BOOK-BASH-R2.md),
   843	[`BOOK-BASH-R2.md:253-269`](../BOOK-BASH-R2.md)). This is another formal
   844	warning that a global realizability theorem, rather than another local
   845	state count, is required.
   846	
   847	---
   848	
   849	## 10. The exact global coordinate: mismatch energy
   850	
   851	The existing resolved-pencil calculation identifies precisely where new
   852	mathematics must enter.
   853	
   854	Let
   855	
   856	\[
   857	 \deg f=B\alpha,qquad \deg g=B\beta.
   858	\]
   859	
   860	Resolve the two projective pencils simultaneously. For each proper or
   861	infinitely-near base center \(p\), let \(R_p,S_p\) be the multiplicities of
   862	generic members of the two pencils, using zero when a center belongs only to
   863	one. In the orthogonal total-transform basis,
   864	
   865	\[
   866	 C=B\alpha H-\sum_pR_pE_p^*,\qquad
   867	 D=B\beta H-\sum_pS_pE_p^*.
   868	\]
   869	
   870	Because general pencil fibers have square zero and meet in the affine plane
   871	in `td` points,
   872	
   873	\[
   874	 \sum_pR_p^2=B^2\alpha^2,qquad
   875	 \sum_pS_p^2=B^2\beta^2,qquad
   876	 \sum_pR_pS_p=B^2\alpha\beta-td.
   877	\]
   878	
   879	Expanding the square gives the **PROVED** identity
   880	
   881	\[
   882	 \boxed{
   883	 \frac{td}{\alpha\beta}
   884	 =\frac12\sum_p
   885	 \left(\frac{R_p}{\alpha}-\frac{S_p}{\beta}\right)^2.} \tag{10.1}
   886	\]
   887	
   888	Combining with the pole formula gives
   889	
   890	\[
   891	 \boxed{
   892	 \sum_P\frac{a_Pb_P}{\nu_P}
   893	 =\frac12\sum_p
   894	 \left(\frac{R_p}{\alpha}-\frac{S_p}{\beta}\right)^2.} \tag{10.2}
   895	\]
   896	
   897	Equivalently, for
   898	
   899	\[
   900	 \Delta:=C/\alpha-D/\beta,
   901	\]
   902	
   903	\[
   904	 \Delta^2=-\frac{2td}{\alpha\beta}.                   \tag{10.3}
   905	\]
   906	
   907	The derivation is recorded in
   908	[`xmodel/sol-tdbound-review.md:349-427`](sol-tdbound-review.md). No local
   909	identification of an individual pole summand with an individual resolution
   910	center is currently proved.
   911	
   912	### Minimal necessary-and-sufficient missing lemma
   913	
   914	> **CONJECTURE KME-2 (Keller mismatch-energy bound).** For every
   915	> Sigray-normalized polynomial Keller pair of type \((\alpha,\beta)\), on a
   916	> common resolution of its two projective pencils,
   917	> \[
   918	>   \sum_p\left(\frac{R_p}{\alpha}
   919	>                    -\frac{S_p}{\beta}\right)^2\le2. \tag{KME-2}
   920	> \]
   921	
   922	By (10.1)--(10.3), KME-2 is exactly equivalent to
   923	
   924	\[
   925	 \sum_P\frac{a_Pb_P}{\nu_P}\le1,qquad
   926	 td\le\alpha\beta,qquad \Delta^2\ge-2.              \tag{10.4}
   927	\]
   928	
   929	This is the minimal exact lemma: neither weaker nor stronger language is
   930	hidden in the reduction.
   931	
   932	**Hardness: 9/10.** The exceptional lattice alone does not imply KME-2.
   933	Even support on an ADE configuration is insufficient: multiples of a root
   934	have arbitrarily negative square. The theorem must constrain the
   935	coefficients of the normalized difference divisor, using the constant
   936	Jacobian and proximity relations at the centers where normalized
   937	multiplicities diverge.
   938	
   939	### A more structural sufficient target
   940	
   941	The following statement is stronger than KME-2 but gives a concrete bridge
   942	from common-power contacts to the required global inequality.
   943	
   944	> **CONJECTURE PCC (proportional-center coverage).** There are distinct
   945	> resolved centers and nonnegative integers \(h_p\), compatible with the
   946	> proximity cluster, such that
   947	> \[
   948	> R_p\ge\alpha h_p,qquad S_p\ge\beta h_p,qquad
   949	> \sum_ph_p^2\ge B^2-1.                               \tag{PCC}
   950	> \]
   951	
   952	If PCC holds, then
   953	
   954	\[
   955	 I_\infty=\sum_pR_pS_p
   956	 \ge\alpha\beta\sum_ph_p^2
   957	 \ge\alpha\beta(B^2-1),
   958	\]
   959	
   960	and hence `td <= alpha*beta`. Corollary 7.4's common powers are plausible
   961	sources of the \(h_p\), but the needed weighted/Laurent-to-projective
   962	transport, proximity accounting, distinct-center coverage, and no-double-
   963	counting theorem do not exist in the repository.
   964	
   965	**Hardness: 8.5/10.** This is the most concrete proof lane found. FC5 could
   966	participate only after an emission-to-proximity theorem says which base
   967	centers and which source increments a chart represents. Its current pair
   968	`(w,M)` does not contain that information.
   969	
   970	### The smallest sector lemma an emission proof must import
   971	
   972	> **CONJECTURE SP (single-pole cap).** If a one-pole Sigray configuration is
   973	> globally realizable by a polynomial Keller pair, then
   974	> \[
   975	>   ab\le\nu.                                           \tag{SP}
   976	> \]
   977	
   978	SP is exactly TD-BOUND in the one-pole sector. It is necessary for any
   979	merge-based proof, because there is no multi-leaf merge in that sector. The
   980	family in Proposition 3 satisfies the current local rules while violating SP
   981	by an unbounded factor.
   982	
   983	**Hardness: 8/10.** A proof must use global gluing/algebraization or force
   984	unbooked charge. Refining FC5 cannot prove SP because FC5 has no incoming
   985	merge to compare.
   986	
   987	### Relaxed finite typewise target
   988	
   989	Even a nonsharp finite bound would require:
   990	
   991	> **CONJECTURE KME-finite.** For each fixed \((\alpha,\beta)\), there is an
   992	> effective constant \(C_{\alpha,\beta}<\infty\), independent of the degree
   993	> scale and tree, such that
   994	> \[
   995	>   \sum_p\left(\frac{R_p}{\alpha}
   996	>                    -\frac{S_p}{\beta}\right)^2
   997	>       \le C_{\alpha,\beta}.                          \tag{KME-finite}
   998	> \]
   999	
  1000	Then
  1001	
  1002	\[
  1003	 td\le\frac{\alpha\beta}{2}C_{\alpha,\beta}.          \tag{10.5}
  1004	\]
  1005	
  1006	Proposition 3 shows that KME-finite is not encoded in the current local
  1007	laws even for type `(2,3)`. Its proof would already be new global geometry.
  1008	
  1009	---
  1010	
  1011	## 11. Why a universal absolute `td` bound is not an intermediate theorem
  1012	
  1013	### Proposition 4 (iteration obstruction)
  1014	
  1015	**PROVED.** Let \(F:\mathbb A^2\to\mathbb A^2\) be a nonautomorphic Keller
  1016	map with \(td(F)=d>1\). Then for every \(k\ge1\), \(F^k\) is a
  1017	nonautomorphic Keller map and
  1018	
  1019	\[
  1020	 td(F^k)=d^k.                                          \tag{11.1}
  1021	\]
  1022	
  1023	The Jacobian determinant follows from the chain rule. For topological
  1024	degree, the pullback \(F^*\) embeds the rational function field into itself,
  1025	and
  1026	
  1027	\[
  1028	 [K:(F^k)^*K]
  1029	 =\prod_{j=0}^{k-1}[(F^j)^*K:(F^{j+1})^*K]
  1030	 =d^k.                                                 \tag{11.2}
  1031	\]
  1032	
  1033	If some \(F^k\) were an automorphism, its inverse composed with
  1034	\(F^{k-1}\) would give a polynomial inverse for \(F\), a contradiction.
  1035	
  1036	Consequently:
  1037	
  1038	> **PROVED LOGICAL COROLLARY.** For any fixed finite \(N\), the statement
  1039	> “every plane Keller counterexample has `td <= N`” implies that there are no
  1040	> plane Keller counterexamples. Conversely, if the Jacobian conjecture is
  1041	> true, that bound is vacuously true. Thus existence of a universal absolute
  1042	> bound is logically equivalent to JC2, not a weaker intermediate rung.
  1043	
  1044	This does not obstruct a bound on a *selected globally minimal*
  1045	counterexample. Iteration is precisely why that quantifier must be stated.
  1046	
  1047	---
  1048	
  1049	## 12. TD-BOUND alone does not make the book ladder cofinal
  1050	
  1051	The sharp conjecture gives only
  1052	
  1053	\[
  1054	 td\le\alpha\beta.                                    \tag{12.1}
  1055	\]
  1056	
  1057	The current GGV Algorithm 8 catalog is finite only after an explicit input
  1058	degree/valuation bound. It is not an absolute finite list of possible types.
  1059	This is stated in
  1060	[`REDUCTION.md:225-267`](../REDUCTION.md) and
  1061	[`REDUCTION.md:887-904`](../REDUCTION.md), and reiterated in
  1062	[`xmodel/sol-tdbound-review.md:279-325`](sol-tdbound-review.md).
  1063	
  1064	Therefore the cofinality sentence in `TDBOUND.md:97-103` is not currently a
  1065	theorem. A second global compactness statement is required:
  1066	
  1067	> **CONJECTURE SC (selected-counterexample type cap).** If a plane Keller
  1068	> counterexample exists, the globally minimal GGV pair selected for the
  1069	> reduction can be chosen so that its transported Sigray type satisfies
  1070	> \[
  1071	>   \alpha\beta\le B_0                              \tag{SC}
  1072	> \]
  1073	> for one effective absolute constant \(B_0\).
  1074	
  1075	KME-2 plus SC would give `td <= B_0` for that selected pair and would avoid
  1076	the iteration obstruction, because it does not claim a bound for every
  1077	iterate. A direct selected-pair `td` bound would serve the same purpose.
  1078	
  1079	**Hardness: 10/10.** No current GGV or sheet theorem supplies an upper type
  1080	or degree bound. In addition, cofinal enumeration still needs the
  1081	full-configuration landing gaps recorded in `REDUCTION.md:870-904`, notably
  1082	post-jump `M>=2` suffixes and mixed later merges.
  1083	
  1084	---
  1085	
  1086	## 13. Final dependency ledger
  1087	
  1088	| desired conclusion | what the present inputs prove | exact missing item | rating |
  1089	|---|---|---|---:|
  1090	| FC5 state after a chart | equation (1.1), type- and coefficient-independent | none | proved |
  1091	| finite depth quotient at fixed entry | finite `w`/jump menu on `M=1` ancestry | `M>=2` and full-history closure | existing perimeter |
  1092	| finite coefficient quotient at fixed schema | at most `(Qhat-1)!` isolated types | zero-dimensionality and cross-schema properness | existing perimeter |
  1093	| single-pole mass `<=1` | no bound; Proposition 3 is locally unbounded | **CONJECTURE SP** | 8/10 |
  1094	| full pole mass `<=1` | exact reduction to mismatch energy | **CONJECTURE KME-2** or sufficient PCC | 9/10 |
  1095	| any finite bound for fixed type | no bound from local laws | **CONJECTURE KME-finite** | 9/10 |
  1096	| absolute cofinal book ladder | not supplied by TD-BOUND or GGV catalog | **CONJECTURE SC** plus full landing | 10/10 |
  1097	| universal absolute bound on every counterexample | would contradict iterates of any one counterexample | equivalent to JC2 | 10/10 |
  1098	
  1099	## Conclusion
  1100	
  1101	The merged-emission law is valuable as a deterministic transition rule, but
  1102	it is not the missing global accountant. Its exact pole-mass interface is
  1103	\(\Phi_s\); that potential is nonmonotone on coefficient-solvable charts,
  1104	and FC5 can erase an arbitrarily large fixed-type scale. Depth closure and
  1105	NF-M then quotient what remains only after the unbounded entry/schema
  1106	parameters have been fixed.
  1107	
  1108	The minimum honest next theorem is not another emission identity. It is a
  1109	global Keller/proximity statement at divergence centers--KME-2 for the sharp
  1110	bound, or KME-finite for any typewise bound--together with SP to cover the
  1111	merge-blind one-pole sector. Absolute book cofinality additionally requires a
  1112	selected-counterexample type cap and full-configuration landing.
  1113	
  1114	No `td` upper bound, sharp or weak, is proved here. The new proved output is
  1115	the obstruction: **the present FC5 + depth + NF-M bookkeeping is
  1116	noncoercive, even at fixed type, and the precise missing information is the
  1117	global normalized mismatch/proximity energy.**

